<common-name datatype='string'>predicted structural protein, ethanolamine utilization microcompartment</common-name>
<comment datatype='string'>The EutL protein resembles carboxysomal shell proteins and is thought to form a proteinaceous membrane that confines a microcompartment for ethanolamine ammonia-lyase [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/19451619&quot;&gt;Sagermann09&lt;/a&gt;].  The flat, cyclic pseudohexameric structure with a central pore may be a basic building block for a microcompartment shell [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/20044574&quot;&gt;Tanaka10a&lt;/a&gt;].  The pore structure can be triggered to open upon exposure to zinc [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/20851901&quot;&gt;Takenoya10&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
Crystal structures of EutL have been solved at 2.2 &amp;Aring; [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/19194002&quot;&gt;Nikolakakis09&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/19451619&quot;&gt;Sagermann09&lt;/a&gt;], 2.3 &amp;Aring; [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/20044574&quot;&gt;Tanaka10a&lt;/a&gt;] and 2.6 &amp;Aring; [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/20851901&quot;&gt;Takenoya10&lt;/a&gt;] resolution.  The structures show that the protein is arranged in a hexagonal trimeric structure, with the monomers arranged around a large central pore that is either closed [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/19451619&quot;&gt;Sagermann09&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/20044574&quot;&gt;Tanaka10a&lt;/a&gt;] or open [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/20044574&quot;&gt;Tanaka10a&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/20851901&quot;&gt;Takenoya10&lt;/a&gt;].  In solution, EutL initially elutes as a trimer during size-exclusion chromatography, but forms larger complexes at lower pH or by longer incubation time after cell lysis [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/19451619&quot;&gt;Sagermann09&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
The functions of the orthologous &lt;i&gt;eut&lt;/i&gt; operon genes for ethanolamine utilization in &lt;i&gt;Salmonella typhimurium&lt;/i&gt; have been studied experimentally [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/10464203&quot;&gt;Kofoid99&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/16585748&quot;&gt;Penrod06&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
A &lt;i&gt;eutL&lt;/i&gt; up allele increases fitness during growth in medium containing lignocellulosic hydrolysate [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/20639866&quot;&gt;Warner10&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
Comment: [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/20044564&quot;&gt;Kang10c&lt;/a&gt;]
</comment>
